# KNOWN GAP: `.iter().enumerate()` over a Set PANICS the compiler — and it is
# the exact spelling the compiler's own error message tells users to write.
#
# Round XXVII Track D rejects a direct `s.enumerate()` with
# `error[E_EnumerateOnNonIterator]: use `.iter().enumerate()`` — correct in
# principle (Set/HashSet impl `Iterable[T]`, not `Iterator[T]`, and
# `.enumerate()` is an Iterator adapter). But the advised remedy does not
# compile: the enumerate adapter reads field index 2 of the iterator struct,
# while `SetIter__T` has only 2 fields, so lir-lowering emits invalid LIR.
#
# MEASURED AT HEAD (2026-08-03), `gg build`:
#   thread 'gg-main' panicked at src/lir/validate.rs:137:13:
#   LIR invariant violated after lir-lowering:
#     in @main bb0: field index 2 out of range for SetIter__int64_t (has 2 fields)
#
# The LIR validator is doing its job — this is a genuine invariant violation
# caught before emit, not a validator false positive. The defect is upstream:
# the enumerate adapter assumes the 3-field (ptr/index/len) ArrayIter shape.
#
# Note the probe discipline (AGENTS.md Core #15(d)): read this off `gg build`,
# never `gg run` — `gg run` masks the abort as a silent exit.
#
# CONTROLS (both work at HEAD, so this fixture isolates the adapter, not Set
# iteration or the destructuring syntax):
#   - `for x in s.iter():`                      → prints 7, 8
#   - `for (i, x) in v.enumerate():` on Vector  → prints 0->7, 1->8
#
# INTENDED: this compiles and prints the two lines below. `Set[T]` is
# insertion-ordered (D38), so the indices are meaningful and deterministic.
# Graduate out of known_gaps/ the round the adapter is fixed.
int main():
    Set[int] s = {}
    s.add(7)
    s.add(8)
    for (i, x) in s.iter().enumerate():
        print(f"{i}->{x}")
    return 0
